    "title": "Is ‘SNL’ New Tonight (April 18, 2026)? Host, Musical Guest Details",
    "text": "Here’s everything you need to know about this week’s ‘Saturday Night Live’ episode.Apr 18, 2026 5:00 PM EDTJack Black, Tina FeyPhoto by NBC on Getty ImagesKey PointsNo new SNL episode airs this week; the show is taking a break.Olivia Rodrigo, Matt Damon, and Will Ferrell will host the final three episodes.SNL new episodes air Saturdays at 11:30 p.m. EST on NBC and Peacock. It seems like just yesterday that Saturday Night Live‘s 51st season got off to an unforgettable start (thanks in no small part to host Bad Bunny). Believe it or not, we’re already in the final weeks of this standout season…so we need these last few episodes to keep us going while we wait for the next go-round. At this point, you’re probably wondering…is there a new episode of SNL tonight? Here’s everything you need to know before you tune in! Is there a new episode of ‘SNL’ tonight? Unfortunately, no. SNL is taking this week off. Last week, on April 11, Academy Award-nominated actor Colman Domingo hosted Saturday Night Live for the first time. Domingo was joined by Grammy-nominated Brazilian singer-songwriter Anitta as musical guest, also making her SNL debut. Who is hosting next week and who is the musical guest? SNL will be taking the week of April 25 off. What time does ‘SNL’ air? SNL airs at 11:30 p.m. EST / 8:30 p.m. PST. Where can I watch ‘SNL’? New episodes of SNL air live on NBC and Peacock on Saturdays (they’re available to stream later). Who else is hosting and performing on ‘SNL’ this season? The final three episodes of SNL Season 51 will kick off on May 2 when Olivia Rodrigo pulls double duty as both host and musical guest. On May 9, Matt Damon will return to SNL for his third time as host, joined by Noah Kahan as musical guest; May 16’s episode will be led by Will Ferrell as host and Paul McCartney as musical guest. About the authorJacqueline Burt Cote has been writing about entertainment (and much more) since the days when print newspapers and magazines reigned supreme, 